###################################################################################################
SETUP
###################################################################################################

The image processing procedures use a command line interface. A series of arguments must be passed-
see the comments at the top of image_processing_procedures.py for more information, including
examples.

One of the required arguments is the directory where you have installed FIJI.app. You
must install Fiji so that you can access ImageJ since this process relies on running ImageJ using
Java locally. This means you must also run Java.

It may be possible to run things online using Maven and avoid a local Fiji install, but I couldn't
get this working. Hence why I've made this all dependent on a Fiji install.

I do not know if this works for mac or linux. It should work for Windows 11 as of September 2026.

1.  Install Java 11 (Azul Zulu 11.XX.XXX) from https://www.azul.com/downloads/?package=jdk#zulu

2.  Extract it to somewhere permanent, e.g. C:\Program Files\Zulu\zulu-11

3.  Make sure that you have the filepath zulu-11\bin\java.exe

4.  Download maven from https://maven.apache.org/download.cgi. I downloaded 3.9.X, now 3.10.X is
    out. It shouldn't matter. This may be unnecessary.

5.  Extract it to somewhere permanent, e.g. C:\Program Files\Apache\maven

6.  Make sure that you have the filepath maven\bin\mvn.cmd

7.  Edit your system environmental variables. Edit the system environment variables -> Environment
    Variables -> System variables. Set JAVA_HOME to your zulu directory from before. Using the same
    example as before it would be C:\Program Files\Zulu\zulu-11. Set MAVEN_HOME to your maven
    directory from before. Using the same example as before, it would be
     C:\Program Files\Apache\maven

8.   Add these to Path: %JAVA_HOME%\bin , %MAVEN_HOME%\bin

9.  Check that that's all working by opening a terminal and running "java -version". You should get
     11.X.X. Then run "mvn -version". It should also say that you're using Java 11.

10. Download and install Fiji from https://imagej.net/software/fiji/downloads. Version shouldn't
    matter, just whatever's Windows 64 bit.

11. Make sure that the path Fiji.app\ImageJ-win64.exe exists. Write down the path to get there,
    e.g. C:\Users\Me\Fiji.app. This is vital.

12. Make sure that your venv has all of the required modules installed, including pyimagej. If you
    get an error like "jar tool not found at ...cjdk\cache...jre...bin\jar.exe" it's caused by the
    pyimagej install also installing its own Java runtime instead of using the one we installed.
    Stick this in the terminal to fix it: $env:JAVA_HOME = "C:\Program Files\Zulu\zulu-11"

###################################################################################################
OUTPUTS
###################################################################################################
The outputs of this proces are three JSON files- one with information about each particle, one with
whole-of-image soiling information, and one with masking thresholds. Intermediary images are also
produced.

##########
The particle information JSON contains the following data for every particle identified:

label:
A unique identifier for each particle, starting at 1 for the first particle in the list.

area_px:
The number of pixels that constitute the particle, as identified from the mask using 8-neighbour
connectivity.

area_um2:
Directly proportional to the area in pixels- number of pixels multiplied by the square of the
microns-per-pixel scale of the image.

effective_diameter_um2:
Directly proportional to the square root of the area_um2 of the particle- converted using
A=pi*(d/2)**2.

centroid_px:
Tuple (row, col) of the coordinate that the centroid of the particle is located at.

perimeter_px:
The approximate perimeter of the particle, measured as number of edges less (2-sqrt(2)) for each
non-adjacent corner. See https://imagejdocu.list.lu/faq/technical/what_are_the_algorithms_used_to_calculate_particle_area_and_perimeter

eccentricity:
Eccentricity of the ellipse that best represents the particle. sqrt(1-(min_ax/maj_ax)**2)

major_axis_length_um:
Major axis of the best-fitting ellipse. Source code:
https://github.com/imagej/ImageJ/blob/master/ij/process/EllipseFitter.java

minor_axis_len_um:
Minor axis of the best-fitting ellipse. Source code:
https://github.com/imagej/ImageJ/blob/master/ij/process/EllipseFitter.java

spheroid_volume_um3:
Calculates volume of a spheroid using the minor and major axes. 

bbox_px:
Tuple (min_row, min_col, max_row, max_col) that bounds the particle.

##########
The whole-of-image soiling JSON contains the following data:

soil_volume_um3:
Sum of the spheroid volume of each particle.

soil_area_um2:
Sum of the area of each particle

soil_area_coverage_decimal:
Sum of the area of each particle over total canvas area

soil_volume_um3_per_m2:
Soil volume divided by 1 square metre

##########
The thresholds JSON contains a list of lists of threshold values. Each list belongs to one mask and
contains the upper and lower threshold for a pixel to be deemed inside of a particle.

###################################################################################################
IMAGES
###################################################################################################
An image of the mask produced at each stage, an image of the final combined mask, an image of the
particle mapping overlayed on the original microscope image, and a histogram of effective diameters
are all produced and placed in the same folder as the JSONs.